              "text": "**\"Still in Love with You\"** is a classic country song by the legendary duo **Brooks & Dunn**. \n\n### Key Details:\n* **Album:** *Brand New Man* (Brooks & Dunn's incredibly successful 1991 debut studio album)\n* **Released:** August 13, 1991\n* **Writers:** Kix Brooks, Ronnie Dunn, and Don Cook\n* **Lead Vocals:** Ronnie Dunn \n\n### About the Song:\nThough it wasn't released as a major radio single (like fellow album tracks \"Neon Moon\", \"Boot Scootin' Boogie\", or the title track \"Brand New Man\"), \"Still in Love with You\" is a favorite among longtime fans of the duo. It is a slow, melancholic, neotraditional country ballad about having to say a difficult goodbye. The narrator is getting into a taxi to leave, but realizes that no matter where he goes or what he dreams of, he is still completely in love with the person he is leaving behind.\n\n---\n\n### **\"Still in Love with You\" Lyrics**\n\n**(Verse 1)**\nMy taxicab's waiting\nWish I could hold you one last time\nAfter all we had together\nThere's gotta be a better way\nOf telling you goodbye\n\n**(Chorus)**\nIf I had a horse\nI'd ride off in the sunset\nIf I had wings, I'd fly off\nIn the sky so blue\nIf I had the time, I'd spend my\nWhole life dreaming\nBut I'd wake up, still in love with you\n\n**(Verse 2)**\nBe in Baton Rouge by morning\nHope you're missing me by then\nI'd like to be the kind of memory\nThat makes you want to take me back\nIn your arms again\n\n**(Chorus)**\nIf I had a horse\nI'd ride off in the sunset\nIf I had wings, I'd fly off\nIn the sky so blue\nIf I had the time, I'd spend my\nWhole life dreaming\nBut I'd wake up, still in love with you\n\n**(Bridge)**\nSome night when you get lonely\nWill you still remember me?\n\n**(Chorus)**\nIf I had a horse\nI'd ride off in the sunset\nIf I had wings, I'd fly off\nIn the sky so blue\nIf I had the time, I'd spend my\nWhole life dreaming\nBut I'd wake up, still in love with you\nYes, I'd wake up, still in love with you",
